e] [17592186115234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567056 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567074 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567077 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567100 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567017 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567034 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115336 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567028 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567079 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115651 1071 "![](images/ef/ef8e883c83a32ae5ece92686a73ec1c1210d92074d33d4a2e44572add51a88ee.png)\n\nOn which interval(s) is the function $y = f(x)$, shown above, increasing?" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186115651 1071 "![](images/ef/ef8e883c83a32ae5ece92686a73ec1c1210d92074d33d4a2e44572add51a88ee.png)\n\nOn which interval(s) is the function $y=f(x)$, shown above, increasing?" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186115651 1074 "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (0,1)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ the graph rises\n\nThe function is increasing on the intervals where it never falls: $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186116491 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y &={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186116491 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186123494 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123497 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186124000 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186124001 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186124002 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186124003 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566377 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566430 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566515 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186123998 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566641 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186123998 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566641 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186124581 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567108 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186125149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186125149 1071 "Let $f(x) = a\\sqrt{x + b} + c$. Now consider the following properties of $f(x)$:\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x ∈ [1, ∞)$.\n- The range is $f(x) ∈ [2, ∞)$.\n- The point $(5, 4)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$.\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186125149 1071 "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{x+b}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The range is $f(x)\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The point $(5,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186125149 1074 "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [1,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-b=1\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{x-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(5,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =a\\sqrt{5-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =2a+2 \\\\ 2a & =2 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}+2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2=2.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186125233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186125233 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3x + 1} - 2$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186125233 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt{3x+1}-2.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186125233 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x+1}-2=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3x+1}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3x+1})^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 3x+1 & =4 \\\\ 3x & =3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 1+1}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186126026 1078 #edb/ref 17592186126028 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186128605 1078 #edb/ref 17592186128608 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567015 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567043 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567086 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129220 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567097 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129220 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567105 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567009 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567059 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567098 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567101 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129421 1071 "Let $R = [2, 4] \\cdot [1, 5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 5$ for all partitions $P$ of $R$, and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186129421 1071 "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=5$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129421 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}5\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =5({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =5(4-2)(5-1) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=40\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 40=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=40.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129854 1071 "Let $R = [2, 5] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y)$ be a function defined over $R$.\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$L(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(2y_{j - 1} - 2x_{i} + 1)Δx_{i}Δy_{j},; U(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(2y_{j} - 2x_{i - 1} + 1)Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$, compute $∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n$∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + x_{i})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} = (x_{m}^{2} - x_{0}^{2})(y_{n} - y_{0}); ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δx_{i}Δy_{j} = (x_{m} - x_{0})(y_{n} - y_{0})$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186129854 1071 "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186129854 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1\\le 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+1\\le 2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1\\le ({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+1\\le 2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+1]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{j-1}+{x}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({5}^{2}-{2}^{2})(2-(-2))=84 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({2}^{2}-(-2)^{2})(5-2)=0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =(5-2)(2-(-2))=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 0-84+12\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le -72\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-72.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17