d net, shown below. What will be the total surface area of the pyramid?\n\n![](images/29/29c918514e60da13363883770befd9d80098deaba0b45cfcafa3255feb143261.png)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186147298 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a triangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ triangular base and $3$ triangular sides.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot (Area of base) + 3 \\cdot (Area of side) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 10 + 3 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 10 + 36 \\\\\n&= 46in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186147298 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a triangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ triangular base and $3$ triangular sides.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of base})+3\\times (\\text{Area of side}) \\\\  & =1\\times 10+3\\times 12 \\\\  & =10+36 \\\\  & =46\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147304 1071 "![](images/fe/fe43d65b35d583b21f25977a738e758a31d6b04eb08957877879cc54eef3f45f.png)\n\nBy assuming that all of the side faces have the same height $(8\\,\\text{in})$, find the total surface area of the square pyramid above by considering its net." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186147304 1071 "![](images/fe/fe43d65b35d583b21f25977a738e758a31d6b04eb08957877879cc54eef3f45f.png)\n\nBy assuming that all of the side faces have the same height $(8\\,\\text{in}),$ find the total surface area of the square pyramid above by considering its net." 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147304 1074 "The net of our pyramid is as follows:\n\n![](images/c4/c4a5fe995aaaf47b7ab2b90492059d739fc2352955dc1e12a71852fc13046a33.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with sides equal to $5 \\, \\text{in}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of square} = 5 \\times 5 = 25 \\, \\text{in}^2\n$$\n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $5\\,\\text{in}$ and the height is $8\\,\\text{in}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 5 \\times 8 = 20 \\, \\text{in}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (the base) and $4$ triangles (the side faces).\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot (Area of the square) + 4 \\cdot (Area of the triangle) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 25 + 4 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 25 + 80 \\\\\n&= 105in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186147304 1074 "The net of our pyramid is as follows:\n\n![](images/c4/c4a5fe995aaaf47b7ab2b90492059d739fc2352955dc1e12a71852fc13046a33.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with sides equal to $5 \\, \\text{in}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of square} = 5 \\times 5 = 25 \\, \\text{in}^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $5\\,\\text{in}$ and the height is $8\\,\\text{in}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 5 \\times 8 = 20 \\, \\text{in}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (the base) and $4$ triangles (the side faces). \n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of the square})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the triangle}) \\\\  & =1\\times 25+4\\times 20 \\\\  & =25+80 \\\\  & =105\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567033 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147622 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567037 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147669 1050 420.0 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186147907 1074 "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ squares (base and top) and $4$ rectangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =2\\times 9+4\\times 12 \\\\  & =18+48 \\\\  & =66\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186148726 1064 "The **surface area** of a polyhedron is the combined area of all its faces.\n\nTo calculate the surface area of a polyhedron, we use nets.\n\nLet's consider the net of the rectangular pyramid below. What is the surface area of this pyramid?\n\n![](images/7f/7f1843bc6f3c0e331b99045c731368771f831abdccdb7c255606d39a0fb616e4.png)\n\nThe surface area of our pyramid consists of one rectangle (the base) and four triangles (the side faces).\n\nAlso, notice that there are two different triangular faces:\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $60\\,\\text{in}^2$, and\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $30\\,\\text{in}^2$.\n\nCalculating the total area, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot 120in^{2} + 2 \\cdot 60in^{2} + 2 \\cdot 30in^{2} \\\\\n&= 120in^{2} + 120in^{2} + 60in^{2} \\\\\n&= 300in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the surface area of the pyramid is $300 \\, \\text{in}^2$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186148726 1064 "The **surface area** of a polyhedron is the combined area of all its faces. \n\nTo calculate the surface area of a polyhedron, we use nets. \n\nLet's consider the net of the rectangular pyramid below. What is the surface area of this pyramid?\n\n![](images/7f/7f1843bc6f3c0e331b99045c731368771f831abdccdb7c255606d39a0fb616e4.png)\n\nThe surface area of our pyramid consists of one rectangle (the base) and four triangles (the side faces). \n\nAlso, notice that there are two different triangular faces:\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $60\\,\\text{in}^2,$ and\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $30\\,\\text{in}^2.$\n\nCalculating the total area, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times 120\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+2\\times 60\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+2\\times 30\\,{\\text{in}}^{2} \\\\  & =120\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+120\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+60\\,{\\text{in}}^{2} \\\\  & =300\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the surface area of the pyramid is $300 \\, \\text{in}^2.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186148727 1064 "If we know a polyhedron's measurements, we can calculate its surface area by first drawing the net of the polyhedron. Then, we calculate the areas of its polygonal faces using known formulas and then sum the results.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the surface area of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85ddfbae506653a6b2c4982be74f9eff2b91df55a5075597ddbbd42a51cc2f52.png)\n\nFirst, we consider the net of our prism.\n\n![](images/98/9874d746b2510403accdbd2e9d77ce8d5bc917b76e68c3f1690a24e8643ad473.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the prism is made up of squares and rectangles.\n\n- The rectangles are $8\\,\\text{ft}$ long and $2\\,\\text{ft}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is\n$\\text{Area of rectangle} = 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\, \\text{ft}^2$.\n- The squares have sides of length $2\\, \\text{ft}$. So, the area of one square is\n$\\text{Area of square} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\, \\text{ft}^2$.\n\nFinally, the surface area of the prism consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 4 \\cdot (Area of rectangle) + 2 \\cdot (Area of square) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 16 + 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 64 + 8 \\\\\n&= 72ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186148727 1064 "If we know a polyhedron's measurements, we can calculate its surface area by first drawing the net of the polyhedron. Then, we calculate the areas of its polygonal faces using known formulas and then sum the results. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the surface area of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85ddfbae506653a6b2c4982be74f9eff2b91df55a5075597ddbbd42a51cc2f52.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we consider the net of our prism.\n\n![](images/98/9874d746b2510403accdbd2e9d77ce8d5bc917b76e68c3f1690a24e8643ad473.png)\n\n      \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the prism is made up of squares and rectangles.\n\n- The rectangles are $8\\,\\text{ft}$ long and $2\\,\\text{ft}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of rectangle} = 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\, \\text{ft}^2.\n$$\n\n- The squares have sides of length $2\\, \\text{ft}.$ So, the area of one square is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of square} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\, \\text{ft}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the prism consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of rectangle})+2\\times (\\text{Area of square}) \\\\  & =4\\times 16+2\\times 4 \\\\  & =64+8 \\\\  & =72\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186175949 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567022 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567102 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179146 1078 #edb/ref 17592186179148 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179158 1078 #edb/ref 17592186179159 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179543 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x}+1$?" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186179543 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x}+1?$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179543 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[4]{1 - 2x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1 - 2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1 - 2x} + 1 & \\le 1 \\\\ f(x) & \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,1]$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3ce03253fe40abe25d69866d5b52a621168e3dab2a45ca33d951f1a4551b98c9.png)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186179543 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt[4]{1-2x}+1 & \\le 1 \\\\ f(x) & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,1].$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3ce03253fe40abe25d69866d5b52a621168e3dab2a45ca33d951f1a4551b98c9.png)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179546 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = -3\\sqrt[3]{5-2x}+6$?" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186179546 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = -3\\sqrt[3]{5-2x}+6?$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179546 1074 "The range of\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nis\n$y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186179546 1074 "The range of $y = \\sqrt[3] x$ is\n$y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567025 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567060 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567099 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186179667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567045 1