owing partitions:\n\n- $P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[0, 2]$\n- $P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[1, 2]$\n\nCalculate $∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)5Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$, where $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186180267 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,2]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,2]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}5Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186180267 1074 "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}5Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,2]$ and $[1,2]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =2-0=2, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=5\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=10.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186180269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186180269 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[0, 2]$\n- $P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[1, 2]$\n\nCalculate $∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)2(x_{i} + x_{i - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$, where $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186180269 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,2]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,2]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}2({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186180269 1074 "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[1,2].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2-1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{n} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}\\,+\\,{x}_{0})({x}_{1}\\,-\\,{x}_{0})\\,+\\,({x}_{2}\\,+\\,{x}_{1})({x}_{2}\\,-\\,{x}_{1})\\,+\\,⋯\\,+\\,({x}_{m}\\,+\\,{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}\\,-\\,{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot 1=8.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186180685 1064 "The range of a radical function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}\n$$\n\ndepends on whether the index $n$ of the radical is even or odd.\n\nLet's start with the odd case first. What is the range of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2\\sqrt[3]{3x-2}+2\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the range of\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nis\n$y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186180685 1064 "The range of a radical function $f(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}$ depends on whether the index $n$ of the radical is even or odd. \n\nLet's start with the odd case first. What is the range of \n\n$$\nf(x) = -2\\sqrt[3]{3x-2}+2?\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the range of $y = \\sqrt[3] x$ is\n$y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTransformations do not change this interval. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186180686 1064 "Let's now focus on the even case. What is the range of\n\n$$\ng(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1}-2\n$$\n\nThe range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply transformations to the inequality above to turn the left-hand side into $g(x)$.\n\nFirst, remember that horizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then subtracting $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[4]{2x - 1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x - 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x - 1} - 2 & \\le - 2 \\\\ g(x) & \\le - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $g(x) \\in (-\\infty,-2]$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b7f3dc07cb72ea087d7cc4ec01379efa212b89fb8ab397b6df5bccfe75e66f5.png)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186180686 1064 "Let's now focus on the even case. What is the range of \n\n$$\ng(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1}-2?\n$$\n\nThe range of $\\sqrt[4] x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4] x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply transformations to the inequality above to turn the left-hand side into $g(x).$\n\nFirst, remember that horizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then subtracting $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1} & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-1}-2 & \\le -2 \\\\ g(x) & \\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $g(x) \\in (-\\infty,-2].$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b7f3dc07cb72ea087d7cc4ec01379efa212b89fb8ab397b6df5bccfe75e66f5.png)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184057 1071 "Find the integral of $x^2 + 1$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186184057 1071 "Find the integral of $x^2 + 1.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184057 1074 "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx^{2} + 1dx &= \\intx^{2}dx + \\int1dx \\\\\n&= \\intx^{2}dx + \\intx^{0}dx \\\\\n&= (\\frac{x^{2 + 1}}{2 + 1}) + x + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186184057 1074 "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1})+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184058 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2 x^3 - x \\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186184058 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2 x^3 - x \\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184058 1074 "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int2x^{3} - xdx &= 2\\intx^{3}dx - \\intxdx \\\\\n&= 2(\\frac{x^{3 + 1}}{3 + 1}) - (\\frac{x^{1 + 1}}{1 + 1}) + C \\\\\n&= 2(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}) - (\\frac{x^{2}}{2}) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{4}}{2} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186184058 1074 "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{3}-x\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1})-(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1})+C \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184059 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left( 5(\\sqrt{x})^{3} - 4 x^2 + 5\\right) \\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184059 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left(5(\\sqrt{x})^{3} - 4 x^2 + 5\\right) \\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186184059 1074 "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(5(\\sqrt{x})^{3} - 4x^{2} + 5)dx &= \\int(5x^{3/2} - 4x^{2} + 5)dx \\\\\n&= 5\\intx^{3/2}dx - 4\\intx^{2}dx + 5\\int1dx \\\\\n&= 5(\\frac{x^{5/2}}{\\frac{5}{2}}) - 4(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}) + 5(x) + C \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}x^{5/2} - \\frac{4}{3}x^{3} + 5x + C \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{x^{5}} - \\frac{4}{3}x^{3} + 5x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186184059 1074 "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5(\\sqrt{x})^{3}-4{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5{x}^{3/2}-4{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{2}\\text{d}x+5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\frac{5}{2}})-4(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+5(x)+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+5x+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+5x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567064 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567073 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567083 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184155 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567046 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186184368 1050 720.0 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186185366 1064 "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $\\displaystyle \\int (x+x^3)\\,\\textrm d x$?\n\nAccording to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\int (f(x) \\pm g(x))\\,\\textrm d x = \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm \\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(x + x^{3})dx &= \\intxdx + \\intx^{3}dx \\\\\n&= (\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + c_{1}) + (\\frac{x^{4}}{4} + c_{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + (c_{1} + c_{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we renamed\n\n$$\nc_1+c_2=C\n$$\n\n(The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186185366 1064 "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $\\displaystyle \\int (x+x^3)\\,\\textrm d x?$ \n\nAccording to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\int (f(x) \\pm g(x))\\,\\textrm d x = \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm d x \\pm \\int g(x) \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1})+(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we renamed $c_1+c_2=C.$ (The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186192468 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 288^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $288^\\circ$, which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/4c/4c60c3aecabe8f625d09843ae5bdbc1e7e5525c1f0e6ab894387320c5420cab8.png)\n\nTo express\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 2π - θ \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{8π}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10π}{5} - \\frac{8π}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 false] [17592186192468 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t288^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $288^\\circ,$ which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/4c/4c60c3aecabe8f625d09843ae5bdbc1