${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+x-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(2)+1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}(2)+(1)-3 \\\\ (1)(2)^{2}-(2)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{11} \\\\ \\frac{17}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.091 \\\\ 1.545\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186204509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186204509 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - y^{4} + 1 \\\\ x^{3}y - 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nC